From a062910778a5055ce63b92729c3782db32f1419c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 22:02:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#130): clip the weave rect to the ancestor clip
 chain, not just the viewport
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A 3D tile inside an `overflow:auto` panel, scrolled out of view WITHIN the
panel, reappeared woven OUTSIDE its container as soon as the PAGE was
scrolled — painted where the container's clip should have removed it
entirely. Same corruption class as browser#117 / patch 0081: the weave
painting a region the page does not own.

THE GOVERNING INVARIANT. The weave may only paint where the browser would
have painted the canvas. A weave rect is therefore the element's screen rect
intersected with EVERY ANCESTOR CLIP.

TWO DIFFERENT NOTIONS OF "CLIPPED", and the fix is only correct because it
applies the second without reintroducing the first.

  1. |draw_properties().visible_layer_rect| — a DRAW property. cc skips
     intersecting with it for kInline3dWeave on purpose (patch 0026): the
     inline-3d tracked chunk lands on a layer with NO drawable content, whose
     visible_layer_rect is EMPTY, so intersecting would zero every weave rect.
     browser#94/#117 then built on the resulting raw element bounds — a tile
     partly visible at the VIEWPORT edge must still report a rect for the
     geometric join to score. That carve-out is hardware-validated at
     90/70/50/10% and is untouched here.

  2. the property-tree CLIP CHAIN — a real `overflow` / `clip-path` clip node
     contributed by an ancestor. This is a live property-tree quantity: it is
     computed and correct whether or not the layer draws, so it is safe on the
     very path that must skip (1). It is what was being ignored, and ignoring
     it is #130: the tracked rect kept its unclipped bounds, so a page scroll
     translated those bounds back into view with nothing left to cut them.

FIX, cc side. CalculateVisibleBounds() gains the ClipTree, and for the weave
feature intersects the projected element rect with the accumulated ancestor
clip. The walk is cc's own idiom from PointIsClippedByAncestorClipNode():
from layer->clip_tree_index() up while `id > kViewportPropertyNodeId`,
honouring AppliesLocalClip() and projecting each node's clip through
transform_tree.ToScreen(clip_node.transform_id) — the same screen space
layer->ScreenSpaceTransform() maps the element rect into.

The viewport clip (node 1) is EXCLUDED, deliberately. That is browser#117's
case and it must stay unclipped here. It also means the change is inert for
an element with no clipping ancestor: the walk starts at the viewport node,
contributes nothing, and the rect is byte-identical to today's.

FIX, viz side — without which the cc half is undone one layer down. An empty
tracked rect used to be DROPPED in SurfaceAggregator::CollectTrackedElementRects.
It must not be, now that empty means "the page paints none of this element":

  * the legacy main-thread channel is a per-element fallback gated on
    CARDINALITY (patch 0094). Dropping the rect lowers the tracked count by
    one, the pigeonhole reads the element as MISSING, and Blink's UNCLIPPED
    getBoundingClientRect rect is admitted in its place — resurrecting the
    exact tile the panel clipped away.
  * the weave already handles an on-list target with nothing on screen: it
    intersects each rect with the window bounds and, when empty, clears the
    target's pending/drawn rects and submits nothing (patch 0028) while
    KEEPING its slot and scratch images, so a tile that comes back pays no
    cold reallocation. Dropping the rect instead starts the absence grace
    period and tears those down.

So a clipped-away tile now travels the same path an off-window tile already
travels. It is inert in the join (browser#94 skips any rect that does not
intersect the root pass), inert in the weave, and it keeps the cardinality
honest. Only an UNIDENTIFIED (zero-token) empty rect is still dropped: it can
hold no target slot and carries no information worth a list entry.

Refs browser#130

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 cc/trees/layer_tree_host_impl.cc              | 91 ++++++++++++++++++-
 .../viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc | 33 ++++++-
 2 files changed, 120 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/cc/trees/layer_tree_host_impl.cc b/cc/trees/layer_tree_host_impl.cc
index dcddff1..5104b64 100644
--- a/cc/trees/layer_tree_host_impl.cc
+++ b/cc/trees/layer_tree_host_impl.cc
@@ -358,6 +358,71 @@ bool IsFixedOrStickyOccluderTransform(int transform_id,
          sticky_or_viewport_scroll_node != outer_scroll_id;
 }
 
+// DisplayXR inline-3D (browser#130): the accumulated ANCESTOR clip that applies
+// to |layer|, in screen space, EXCLUDING the viewport clip.
+//
+// This is deliberately NOT the same thing as |visible_layer_rect|, and the
+// distinction is the whole point of this helper. There are two independent
+// notions of "clipped" in play for a tracked element:
+//
+//   * |draw_properties().visible_layer_rect| — a DRAW property. It is only
+//     computed for layers that contribute to a drawn render surface, so for the
+//     inline-3d tracked chunk (which lands on a layer with no drawable content)
+//     it is EMPTY, and intersecting with it would zero every weave rect. That
+//     is why kInline3dWeave skips it (see |use_unclipped_element_bounds| below,
+//     patch 0026), and why browser#94/#117 rely on the raw element bounds so a
+//     tile that is only partly visible at the VIEWPORT edge still reports the
+//     rect the geometric join is scored against.
+//
+//   * the property-tree CLIP CHAIN — a real `overflow` / `clip-path` clip node
+//     contributed by an ancestor (a scroll container, say). This is a live
+//     property-tree quantity: it exists and is correct whether or not the layer
+//     draws, so it is safe to apply on the very path that must skip
+//     |visible_layer_rect|. Ignoring it is browser#130: a tile scrolled out of
+//     an `overflow:auto` panel kept reporting its unclipped bounds, so a
+//     subsequent PAGE scroll walked those bounds back into view and the weave
+//     painted the tile outside the container that had clipped it away.
+//
+// The viewport clip (node |kViewportPropertyNodeId|) is deliberately excluded:
+// the viewport edge is browser#117's case and must stay unclipped here, exactly
+// as cc's own PointIsClippedByAncestorClipNode() separates the two.
+// |applies| is set false when no ancestor contributes a clip at all, in which
+// case the caller must leave the rect untouched.
+gfx::Rect AncestorClipInScreenSpace(const LayerImpl* layer,
+                                    const ClipTree& clip_tree,
+                                    const TransformTree& transform_tree,
+                                    bool* applies) {
+  *applies = false;
+  gfx::Rect accumulated;
+  for (int id = layer->clip_tree_index(); id > kViewportPropertyNodeId;
+       id = clip_tree.Node(id).parent_id) {
+    const ClipNode& clip_node = clip_tree.Node(id);
+    // A node that does not apply a local clip is a pixel-moving filter, which
+    // EXPANDS the accumulated clip rather than shrinking it. Skipping it is
+    // conservative in the safe direction: we never shrink the weave rect on
+    // account of a node that could only have grown it.
+    if (!clip_node.AppliesLocalClip()) {
+      continue;
+    }
+    // ToEnclosingRect + ProjectEnclosingClippedRect, i.e. rounded OUTWARD, and
+    // the same idiom PointIsClippedByAncestorClipNode() uses on this tree. For
+    // a clip that is the permissive direction: at worst the weave rect keeps a
+    // sub-pixel sliver the page antialiased away, where rounding inward would
+    // shave a pixel off a rect that is entirely correct.
+    const gfx::Rect clip_in_screen_space =
+        MathUtil::ProjectEnclosingClippedRect(
+            transform_tree.ToScreen(clip_node.transform_id),
+            gfx::ToEnclosingRect(clip_node.clip));
+    if (*applies) {
+      accumulated.Intersect(clip_in_screen_space);
+    } else {
+      accumulated = clip_in_screen_space;
+      *applies = true;
+    }
+  }
+  return accumulated;
+}
+
 // Returns the visible bounds of the tracked element on the given layer,
 // optionally subtracting any front occluders in screen space.
 gfx::Rect CalculateVisibleBounds(
@@ -365,6 +430,7 @@ gfx::Rect CalculateVisibleBounds(
     const viz::TrackedElementRect& rect_data_in_layer_space,
     bool use_unclipped_element_bounds,
     const std::vector<HeuristicFixedOrStickyOccluder>& front_occluders,
+    const ClipTree& clip_tree,
     const TransformTree& transform_tree,
     int inner_scroll_id,
     int outer_scroll_id,
@@ -385,6 +451,26 @@ gfx::Rect CalculateVisibleBounds(
   gfx::Rect element_screen_rect = MathUtil::ProjectEnclosingClippedRect(
       layer->ScreenSpaceTransform(), visible_layer_rect);
 
+  // DisplayXR inline-3D (browser#130): having skipped |visible_layer_rect|
+  // above, put back the half of it that IS reachable and IS correct for a
+  // non-drawing layer — the ancestor clip chain. The weave may only paint where
+  // the browser would have painted the canvas, so the weave rect is the
+  // element's screen rect intersected with every ancestor clip. Without this an
+  // `overflow:auto` panel's clip is invisible to the weave and a tile scrolled
+  // out of the panel is woven outside it as soon as the page scrolls.
+  //
+  // An empty result is meaningful and is passed on as such: it says "the page
+  // paints none of this element this frame", which downstream is the same
+  // "nothing on screen for this target" case an off-window tile already takes.
+  if (use_unclipped_element_bounds && !element_screen_rect.IsEmpty()) {
+    bool has_ancestor_clip = false;
+    const gfx::Rect ancestor_clip = AncestorClipInScreenSpace(
+        layer, clip_tree, transform_tree, &has_ancestor_clip);
+    if (has_ancestor_clip) {
+      element_screen_rect.Intersect(ancestor_clip);
+    }
+  }
+
   if (rect_data_in_layer_space.should_exclude_fixed_and_sticky_occlusions) {
     int layer_transform_id = layer->transform_tree_index();
     int layer_sticky_or_viewport_scroll_node =
@@ -2394,6 +2480,9 @@ viz::TrackedElementRects LayerTreeHostImpl::CollectTrackedElementRects(
       outer_scroll ? outer_scroll->transform_id : kInvalidPropertyNodeId;
   const TransformTree& transform_tree =
       active_tree()->property_trees()->transform_tree();
+  // DisplayXR inline-3D (browser#130): the weave rect must honour the ancestor
+  // clip chain, which lives here and stays valid for layers that do not draw.
+  const ClipTree& clip_tree = active_tree()->property_trees()->clip_tree();
   base::flat_map<int, int> sticky_or_viewport_scroll_cache;
   std::vector<HeuristicFixedOrStickyOccluder> front_occluders;
 
@@ -2430,7 +2519,7 @@ viz::TrackedElementRects LayerTreeHostImpl::CollectTrackedElementRects(
           transformed_rect.visible_bounds = CalculateVisibleBounds(
               layer, rect_data,
               feature == viz::TrackedElementFeature::kInline3dWeave,
-              front_occluders, transform_tree, inner_scroll_id,
+              front_occluders, clip_tree, transform_tree, inner_scroll_id,
               outer_scroll_id, sticky_or_viewport_scroll_cache);
 
           // Set the element data with screen space visible bounds.
diff --git a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
index 53026b9..5cbaf7f 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
+++ b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
@@ -2988,11 +2988,38 @@ void SurfaceAggregator::CollectTrackedElementRects(
     if (feature == TrackedElementFeature::kInline3dWeave) {
       // Route into the weave rects; keep these out of the generic map so they
       // don't leak into CopyOutput geometry or the pixel-moving-filter union.
-      // Skip empty rects (fully-clipped/offscreen canvases) — an empty rect
-      // must never suppress the legacy fallback below or reach the weave.
+      //
+      // browser#130: an EMPTY tracked rect used to be dropped here. It must not
+      // be. Now that cc intersects the weave rect with the ancestor clip chain,
+      // "empty" is cc stating positively that the page paints NONE of this
+      // element this frame — a tile scrolled out of its `overflow:auto` panel,
+      // say. Dropping it made that statement indistinguishable from "cc had
+      // nothing to say about this element", and the two are handled in opposite
+      // ways downstream:
+      //
+      //   * the legacy main-thread channel below is a per-element fallback
+      //     gated on CARDINALITY. A dropped rect lowers the tracked count by
+      //     one, the pigeonhole then reads the element as MISSING, and Blink's
+      //     unclipped getBoundingClientRect rect is admitted in its place —
+      //     which resurrects the very tile the panel clipped away, weaving it
+      //     outside its container. The clip fix would have been undone one
+      //     layer down.
+      //   * the weave itself already handles an on-list target with nothing on
+      //     screen: it intersects each rect with the window bounds and, when
+      //     that is empty, clears the target's pending/drawn rects and submits
+      //     nothing (patch 0028) while KEEPING its slot and scratch images, so
+      //     a tile that comes back does not pay a cold reallocation. Dropping
+      //     the rect instead would start the target's absence grace period and
+      //     tear those down.
+      //
+      // So keep it, exactly as an off-window tile is kept. It is inert in the
+      // join (browser#94 already skips any rect that does not intersect the
+      // root pass) and inert in the weave, and it keeps the cardinality honest.
+      // Only an UNIDENTIFIED empty rect is still dropped: with no token it can
+      // hold no target slot and carries no information worth a list entry.
       tracked_metadata_count += tracked_elements.size();
       for (const auto& rect_data : tracked_elements) {
-        if (rect_data.visible_bounds.IsEmpty()) {
+        if (rect_data.visible_bounds.IsEmpty() && rect_data.id.is_zero()) {
           continue;
         }
         // Phase-1 identity: carry the element's token and the (namespace,
